# The Keeper and the Gull

::: {book only}
A story in three short parts.
:::

## I. The storm

[wind against the glass]

Maud had kept the light at Carrick Point for thirty-one years, and in all that time she'd never once been late with the lamp. Tonight the stairs felt longer than usual.

@Maud (to herself) "One hundred and twelve," she counted at the top. "Same as yesterday. Same as always."

A gull sat on the rail outside, feathers flat against the gale. @Gull [screeches]

@Maud "Oh, it's you again." She opened the hatch an inch. @(firmly) "In or out. I'm not heating the whole of Cornwall."

The gull came in, shook itself, and looked at her as if _she_ were the visitor.

@Gull (with great dignity) “I prefer ‘guest’.”

{pause 2s}

Maud sat down rather suddenly on the paraffin can.

***

## II. The song

@Gull (softly) “There was a ship called the _Mary Anne_,
And she sailed from Fowey to Rye;
She carried salt and she carried tin,
And she never came home to dry.”

@Maud “That’s not how it goes.”

@Gull “It’s how _I_ sing it. @[clears throat] Besides, nobody remembers the real one.”

::: @Gull (whispering)
They say the keeper before you heard the same song.
They say she sang it back.
:::

::: hushed
@Maud “Who taught you that?”
@Gull (out loud) “Nobody teaches a gull anything.”
:::

@Maud “Well, I won’t sing it.” She trimmed the wick. [the lamp hisses]

{page}

@Tom “Maud! Maud, are you up there? There’s a light out past the Manacles, and it’s no light of ours.

“I’ve rowed out twice and it moves when I move. I don’t like it, Maud, and you know I don’t scare easy.

“Will you come down?”

@Maud (calling down) 'Give me a minute, Tom!' she shouted. 'And don't you dare row out a third time.'

***

## III. The light

The three of them stood on the gallery: the keeper, the boatman, and the gull, who'd insisted. Tom's lantern swung from a 6" hook by the door.

@Tom (quietly) "There. See it?"

@Gull [chuckles] “That’s no ship. That’s the _Mary Anne_.” @(gently) “She’s come to dry at last.”

Out on the water, the strange light dipped once, as if it bowed, and went out.

@Maud (tired) “Well,” she said, after a long while. “Same as always.” @() “Kettle’s on, if anyone wants it.”

@Gull “Is there toast?”
